now bet you're waking up this morning  the way a lot of us are still in shock  and still can't really wrap our brains  around what happened to Kobe Bryant  yesterday and his 13 year old daughter  who died in that chopper crash when I  first heard the news yesterday my son  sent me a text said he'd heard it  reported on TMZ I said that can't be  true because no one else was reporting  it so you kept hoping against hope that  it was wrong and of course it turned out  to be very very accurate very very sad  yesterday it's the heart one of the  hardest things for me has been looking  at the pictures of Kobe and their  daughter yeah courtside so many of them  they're just I mean it's just my wife  said you said can't look at them anymore  I know I know we're all still crying  today thinking about that news and  that's where we began because the  tributes are pouring in from around the  world to Kobe Bryant he was as you know  an American icon whose cultural impact  really goes far beyond the NBA Bryant  and his 13 year old daughter Gianna and  seven other people were killed yesterday  when their helicopter crashed in  California so this news brought a  massive crowd of people to the Staples  Center and they are still there where  Bryant led the Los Angeles Lakers to  five NBA titles the tributes continued  inside at last night's Grammy Awards  from host Alicia Keys she called it the  house at Kobe Belt she's right and other  performers on Twitter former teammate  Shaquille O'Neal said Bryant was so much  more than an athlete  while Lakers legend Magic Johnson called  him a mentor to both male and female  players President Trump tweeted he loved  his family so much and had such strong  passion for the future and former  President Obama said Bryant was just  getting started in what would have been  just as meaningful a second act we begin  our coverage with Jonathan Vigliotti who  is near the scene of the crash in  Calabasas northwest of downtown Los  Angeles Jonathan what do we know so far  about this investigation Anthony I spoke  with several witnesses who say the fog  was so thick at the time of this crash  they couldn't actually see the  helicopter but they could feel it and  hear it they said it was flying solo it  caused many of the buildings here to  vibrate what ultimately causes crash is  still part of this ongoing investigation  the NTSB is expected here on the scene  later this morning and they do have  their work  cut out for them the crash site is  incredibly steep and there was a  magnesium fire that was sparked  immediately after this crash that we are  told destroyed much of the evidence this  is worldwide isn't just Los Angeles  we're all hurting it's a world of pain  or no he meant happiness basketball  legend Kobe Bryant wasn't just one of  the greatest to play the game he was  admired by people around the world for  his actions off the court as much as  when he was on it God you've got a good  one introduced in the Black Mamba Kobe  bean Brian the retired NBA star was  killed after his personal helicopter  crashed into a steep hillside Sunday  morning killing everybody on board  including his 13 year old daughter  Gianna as you can imagine it's a  logistical nightmare in a sense because  a crash site itself is not easily  accessible debris from the charred  helicopter was scattered across the size  of a football field the helicopter was  flying in fog that was so thick the LAPD  had grounded its helicopter fleet Sunday  morning I think he was just completely  disoriented he couldn't see Scott de  leurs told us he heard the chopper going  down and called 9-1-1 it just slowly  moved over here turning a track right  towards that building and the impact I  counted maybe 20 25 seconds from the  time it was vertical above me in it and  then I heard the impact about 25 seconds  later bryant his daughter and seven  other people were on their way to his  Mamba Cup tournament a Boys and Girls  youth basketball league Bryant found it  after he retired Bryant was to coach his  daughter  Jionni's team among the victims  identified on the helicopter Orange  Coast College baseball coach John  Altobelli  his wife Kerry and daughter Alisa Sarah  Chester and her daughter Payton who was  a teammate of Gianna Bryant's and the  pilot  aras Obion along with the girl's  assistant coach Christina Mouser the  retired Lakers shooting guard often  called his daughter Gianna mamacita  after his own court nicknamed Black  Mamba de fired up about it we'll have a  good time and like her father she  inherited his love of basketball and  hope to one day play in the W  I'm still in charge and still can't  believe  we spoke to a group of players who  participated in the Mamba tournament two  people I wanted to encourage it when I  arrived here at the scene yesterday  morning there were already hundreds of  fans that were gathered here  collectively morning I spoke with one of  them who said when we lose a hero we  gain a sense of our own mortality  and on that note I want to bring in our  National Transportation correspondent  Kris Van Cleave who's following the  investigation Kris what are we learning  well good morning John the pilot was in  touch with air traffic controllers we  know him to be a experienced pilot but  so far there are no reports of any kind  of distress call  at least at this point now the  helicopter was a sikorsky s-76 that is a  respected well known high-end helicopter  with a good safety record the chopper  circled Glendale for about 12 minutes  before continuing on under special  visual flight rules a pilot requesting  special VFR is taking on some added risk  because they are opting to fly at a  lower altitude and at lower visibility  than under normal circumstances that  decision to continue this flight instead  of waiting out the weather is going to  be closely scrutinized remember this  pilot had eight passengers on board and  was flying towards hills that would  require the chopper to gain altitude  despite low level clouds and fog now at  one point air traffic controllers can be  heard telling the pilot he's too low  that was in response to the pilots  request for ATC to follow his flight  path on radar it does not appear at this  point that controllers were telling the  pilot he was flying too low for the  surrounding terrain once the NTSB gets  here on scene they're gonna be looking  for any sign of mechanical failure or  anything else that could have been  brought that could have factored into  bringing this helicopter down Tony all  right Chris a lot of questions about  that fog this morning thank you very  much

